171→## Debugging
172→
173→### Floating Point Precision Errors [[Ep4, 75:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66WGmbykQhI&t=4546s)]
174→```vex
175→float foo = 0;
176→float bar = sin(PI);
177→
178→if(foo == bar) {
179→    @Cd = {1,1,0};
180→} else {
181→    @Cd = {1,0,0};
182→}
183→```
184→Demonstrates floating point precision errors where sin(PI) returns a value extremely close to zero (approximately -8.74228e-8) but not exactly zero, causing the equality comparison to fail. This common numerical issue occurs because floating point numbers have limited precision and trigonometric calculations may introduce small rounding errors.
185→
186→### Floating Point Comparison Issues [Needs Review] [[Ep4, 76:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66WGmbykQhI&t=4562s)]
187→```vex
188→float foo = 0;
189→float bar = sin(@P.y);
190→
191→if(foo == bar) {
192→    @Cd = {1,1,0};
193→} else {
194→    @Cd = {1,0,0};
195→}
196→```
197→Demonstrates a common floating point precision error where sin(@P.y) produces values extremely close to zero (like 0.00000087) but not exactly zero, causing equality comparisons to fail. This illustrates why direct equality testing with floating point numbers is unreliable and should use epsilon-based tolerance checks instead.
198→
199→### Floating Point Precision Errors [[Ep4, 76:04](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66WGmbykQhI&t=4564s)]
200→```vex
201→float foo = 0;
202→float bar = sin(@P);
203→
204→if(foo == bar) {
205→    @Cd = {0,1,0};
206→} else {
207→    @Cd = {1,0,0};
208→}
209→```
210→Demonstrates a floating point precision error where comparing sin(@P) to zero using == fails because floating point calculations can produce values extremely close to zero (like 0.00000087) but not exactly zero. This common issue occurs when testing equality between floating point numbers that should theoretically be equal but have accumulated tiny computational errors.
