458→## Optimization
459→
460→### Compacting Quaternion Code [[Ep7, 35:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9ztkhG7DhuA&t=2116s)]
461→```vex
462→float angle = @Time;
463→vector axis = chv('axis');
464→
465→@orient = quaternion(angle, axis);
466→
467→// Compact version:
468→@orient = quaternion(@Time, chv('axis'));
469→```
470→Demonstrates code refactoring by condensing multi-line quaternion setup into a single compact line. The angle and axis variables are eliminated by directly passing @Time and chv('axis') to the quaternion function, maintaining identical functionality while improving code brevity. This is a stylistic choice that balances readability with conciseness.
471→
472→## Debugging
473→
474→### Storing noise as attribute [[Ep7, 54:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9ztkhG7DhuA&t=3252s)]
475→```vex
476→vector axis;
477→axis = chv('axis');
478→axis = normalize(axis);
479→@a = noise(@P+f@time);
480→axis *= trunc(@a*4)*$PI/2;
481→
